STEP 2 -- `version 1`: a different protocol on the wire, with a measurable consequence
                                                                                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Guide authority, C613-22055-00 REV C p.4, verbatim:
  "Specifying the RIP Version ... By default, RIP only sends and receives RIPv2
  packets. If you need to change this, use the commands below."

  Method: move the DUT and swi_b to version 1 and leave swi_a at version 2, so the same
  change tests both the protocol on the wire and what happens to a neighbour that does
  not follow.

  2a. APPLY (22:11:0x) -- on the DUT and swi_b ONLY:

x960-28YSQ(config)# router rip / version 1 / end        (swi_b)
awplus(config)#     router rip / version 1 / end        (swi_d, the DUT)

  2b. THE PROTOCOL ON THE WIRE (22:11:18, same command, same NIC, 34 s after the v2
  capture):

  22:11:18.909061 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 64, id 5603, flags [DF], proto UDP (17), length 112)
      192.168.10.1.520 > 192.168.10.255.520:
      RIPv1, Response, length: 84, routes: 4
        10.0.0.0,         metric: 1
        192.168.10.0,     metric: 16
        192.168.48.0,     metric: 1
        192.168.70.0,     metric: 2

    Every documented v1/v2 difference, measured side by side on one wire:

    |                        | version 2 (STEP 1e)       | version 1 (2b)                  |
    | ---------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
    | IP destination         | 224.0.0.9 (multicast)     | 192.168.10.255 (broadcast)      |
    | Ethernet destination   | 01:00:5e:.. (multicast)   | ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff               |
    | TTL                    | 1                         | 64                              |
    | header                 | RIPv2                     | RIPv1                           |
    | prefix length / mask   | on every entry            | absent                          |
    | route tag, next-hop    | present                   | absent                          |
    | AFI field              | AFI IPv4                  | absent                          |
    | entries                | 7                         | 4                               |
    | 10.0.41.0/30, 10.0.61.0/30, 10.70.70.1/32, 10.90.90.1/32 | four separate entries | collapsed to one classful 10.0.0.0, metric 1 |
    | 192.168.70.0/24        | /24 explicit              | 192.168.70.0, mask implied by class C |

    The classful collapse is RIPv1 behaving exactly as it must: advertised out vlan10
    (192.168.10.0/24, a different major network), everything inside 10.0.0.0/8 can only
    be summarised to its classful boundary. It is also precisely why the case needed
    192.168.70.0/24 -- a class-C prefix whose natural mask IS /24 survives v1 intact, so
    the v1 leg measures RIP and not a mask collision. The Ethernet broadcast was caught
    independently by the counted leg below, whose capture holds
    `UDP 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.255 :520 [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]`
    where every v2 leg's capture held a multicast one. Two instruments, same answer.

  2c. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE v2 NEIGHBOUR, polled every 30 s:
    22:11:43 .. 22:13:39   R 10.70.70.1/32, R 10.90.90.1/32, R 192.168.70.0/24  (all three)
    22:14:09               R 10.70.70.1/32, R 192.168.70.0/24 -- 10.90.90.1/32 IS GONE
    22:14:39, 22:15:09     unchanged (two routes)

    swi_a's prefix was timed out ~180 s after the DUT went to version 1, exactly the
    `Timeout after 180 seconds` the DUT reports. Confirmed by the DUT's own view
