  Routing Information Sources:
    Gateway       Distance  Last Update  Bad Packets  Bad Routes
    10.0.61.2        120    00:00:27         0            0
                                           <-- 10.0.41.2 has DROPPED OUT of the sources
                                               list entirely
  Number of routes (including connected): 6        (was 7)

    A version-1 receiver does not accept version-2 updates, so the v2-only neighbour
    ages out. Bad Packets and Bad Routes both stayed 0 -- the v2 updates are not counted
    as errors, they are simply not accepted.

  2d. WHAT SURVIVED v1, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY. 10.70.70.1/32 -- a /32 -- is
    STILL learned from swi_b under v1 and is still being refreshed (`show ip rip
    database` at 22:15:57: `R 10.70.70.1/32  10.0.61.2  2  10.0.61.2  vlan61  02:32`,
    the timer resetting). That is correct RIPv1: swi_b advertises it on vlan61, whose
    own address 10.0.61.1/30 is in the SAME major network 10.0.0.0/8, so the entry is
    not summarised at a class boundary and a RIPv1 receiver reads an address with a
    non-zero host part as a HOST route. The DUT could only have derived the /32 that
    way, because v1 carries no mask -- so the /32 in the database IS the evidence of the
    host-route rule. The classful collapse seen in 2b happens on vlan10, a different
    major network: the two observations are the same rule applied to two interfaces, not
    a contradiction.

  2e. THE COUNTED LEG UNDER VERSION 1 (STP re-read 22:16:29, all three ports Designated
    Forwarding), --tag M3-ripv1.  cap 45 frame(s)
      10.70.70.1   : 5 sent, 5 echo replies sourced by 10.70.70.1   -- v1 host route works
      192.168.70.1 : 5 sent, 5 echo replies sourced by 192.168.70.1 -- the class-C prefix
                                                                       works
      10.90.90.1   : 5 sent, 0 echo replies  (5/5 under v2)  <-- THE VERSION MISMATCH,
                     PROVEN IN THE DATAPLANE and not merely as a missing table row
      203.0.113.9  : 5 sent, 0 replies -- control
      192.168.10.1 : 5 sent, 5 replies -- instrument alive
      plus x1 UDP 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.255:520 to ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff -- the v1
      broadcast

  => STEP 2 PASS: `version 1` puts a demonstrably different protocol on the wire --
     broadcast instead of multicast, Ethernet broadcast instead of multicast, TTL 64
     instead of 1, no masks, no tags, and the four 10/8 entries collapsed into one
     classful 10.0.0.0 -- and that difference has a real, selective effect on traffic:
     the v2-only neighbour's prefix aged out after the documented 180 s with 0 bad
     packets and went to 0/5, while the two prefixes reachable under v1 stayed at 5/5 in
     the same leg, with the control dropped and the instrument alive.

