STEP 4 -- withdraw one advertisement, and catch the mechanism, not just the loss
                                                                                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: withdraw ONE prefix and leave the OTHER prefix from the same neighbour, over
  the same link, out of the same port, still advertised. A difference between them
  cannot then be a link, port, neighbour, instrument or bench effect -- only the
  advertisement differs.

  4a. WITHDRAW (swi_b, 09:20:47 UTC = 22:20:47 NZDT):

x960-28YSQ(config)# router rip
x960-28YSQ(config-router)# no network 192.168.70.0/24

  swi_b's lo still carries 192.168.70.1/24 and still has it as a connected route; only
  the RIP advertisement is removed.

  4b. RIP POISONS, IT DOES NOT MERELY TIME OUT. Polled every 15 s, reading BOTH the
  route table and the RIP database, which is where the mechanism is visible:

    22:21:00 (+13 s)  show ip route rip    -> 192.168.70.0/24 ALREADY GONE from the FIB
                      show ip rip database -> R 192.168.70.0/24  10.0.61.2  metric 16
                                                ... vlan61  01:48
    22:21:13          database: metric 16, 01:36
    22:21:28          database: metric 16, 01:21
    22:21:43          database: metric 16, 01:06
    22:21:58          database: metric 16, 00:51
    22:22:13          gone from the database entirely

    Throughout, from the SAME neighbour on the SAME interface:
      R 10.70.70.1/32  10.0.61.2  metric 2  vlan61  02:35-02:53 (timer resetting =
      still being refreshed)

    swi_b sent a TRIGGERED UPDATE AT METRIC 16 (infinity), so the DUT removed the route
    from the FIB within 13 s rather than waiting out the 180 s timeout, and then held it
    in the database for the documented GARBAGE-COLLECT 120 s (01:48 counting down to
    00:00, measured) before deleting it. Both timers match what the DUT prints in
    `show ip protocols rip`: "Timeout after 180 seconds, garbage collect after 120
    seconds". A reading of `show ip route rip` alone would see the route vanish and
    never learn that it was poisoned rather than aged -- the two look identical in the
    route table and take 13 s and 180 s respectively. STEP 2c measured the other
    mechanism on this same bench: swi_a's prefix was NOT poisoned, nobody withdrew it,
    the DUT simply stopped accepting its v2 updates, and it took the full 180 s timeout.

  4c. STATE AT THE COUNTED LEG (22:23:14-22:23:17):

