                                                                     UP and FORWARDING

  4d. THE COUNTED LEG, --tag M5-withdrawn.  cap 44 frame(s)
    192.168.70.1 (withdrawn)                                  : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
                                                                (5/5 in STEP 3)
    10.70.70.1   (SAME NEIGHBOUR, SAME PORT, still advertised) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    10.90.90.1   (other neighbour)                             : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)                                     : 5 sent, 0 replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT)                                  : 5 sent, 5 replies

  Two positive controls, one of them on the very link the withdrawn prefix used.

  4e. RE-ADVERTISE, AND IT COMES BACK (22:23:5x):

x960-28YSQ(config-router)# network 192.168.70.0/24
    22:24:13  R 192.168.70.0/24 [120/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:00:13   <-- re-learned

    --tag M5b-readvertised.  cap 47 frame(s)
      192.168.70.1 : 5/5 sourced by 192.168.70.1   10.70.70.1 : 5/5   10.90.90.1 : 5/5
      203.0.113.9 : 0/5   192.168.10.1 : 5/5

  => STEP 4 PASS: withdraw -> 0/5 -> re-advertise -> 5/5, proven on the wire with the
     tightest control the bench allows -- the other prefix from the same neighbour over
     the same forwarding port stayed at 5/5 in the same leg, so the zero is attributable
     to the withdrawal and to nothing else. The database read also shows HOW RIP did it:
     a triggered update at metric 16 took the route out of the FIB in 13 s, then the
     documented 120 s garbage-collect timer ran it out of the database -- two mechanisms
     that `show ip route` alone cannot tell apart.

